    When built and run
    Then it traps
    And stderr pattern = "L4"

  # the SAME cascade, exited by `exit` — its own argument is read AFTER the replay, so the exit
  # code itself is the LIFO proof (20203 & 0xFF = 235).
  Scenario: defer_cascade_exit (own-native)
    Given source = "cases/defer_cascade_exit.tks"
    When built and run
    Then exit = 235

  Scenario: defer_cascade_exit (C route)
    Given source = "cases/defer_cascade_exit.tks"
    Given env = ["TEKO_BACKEND=c"]
    When built and run
    Then exit = 235

  # step 5's own concern: the diverging-call replay site must not ALSO fire the pre-existing
  # lexical fall-through a second time. A duplicate would report 3, not 2.
  Scenario: defer_not_duplicated_on_diverging_call (own-native)
    Given source = "cases/defer_not_duplicated_on_diverging_call.tks"
    When built and run
    Then exit = 2

  Scenario: defer_not_duplicated_on_diverging_call (C route)
    Given source = "cases/defer_not_duplicated_on_diverging_call.tks"
    Given env = ["TEKO_BACKEND=c"]
    When built and run
    Then exit = 2

  # the documented residual limit (harness doc §18.4): an IMPLICIT panic (div/0 here) has no call
  # site the checker sees, so its guarding defer does NOT run — a CONTRACT, not a gap. The
  # own-native row asserts ONLY the trap: the native backend guards integer division with a raw
  # hardware fault (SIGFPE), not the C route's software `tk_panic_div0` check, so its stderr
  # carries the crash-handler's message, not "deliberate panic" — a PRE-EXISTING, unrelated
  # backend-parity gap (found while writing this fixture; reported, not fixed here).
  Scenario: defer_implicit_panic_documented (own-native)
    Given source = "cases/defer_implicit_panic_documented.tks"
    When built and run
    Then it traps

  Scenario: defer_implicit_panic_documented (C route)
    Given source = "cases/defer_implicit_panic_documented.tks"
    Given env = ["TEKO_BACKEND=c"]
    When built and run
    Then it traps
    And stderr pattern = "teko: deliberate panic: division by zero"

Feature: R0 fat-predicate agreement — a type ALIAS of `str`/`[]T` sizes and stores identically to `str`/`[]T` itself (0.3.1.0 raiz A, crumb R0, 2026-07-29)

  # `type S = <T>` (a type alias) and `str` are terms USED IN PRODUCT SOURCE throughout this
  # compiler (`checker::TypeTable` alone is `type TypeTable = []TypeReg`), so this regression lives
  # HERE, at the project root, never in a channel — the SAME ruling `native process entry` (above)
  # already cites (owner ruling 2026-07-29: a regression of a term the product source itself uses
  # is regressed strictly in `/regressor.tkr`).
  #
  # Measured LIVE on this vagão before the fix (raiz-comum memo §7-quater.3-bis): the layout
  # predicate (`typeexpr_is_fat`, syntactic — asked the ANNOTATION before any resolution) and the
  # store predicate (`is_fat_type`, semantic — asked the field VALUE's resolved type) disagreed on
  # the SAME field the moment that field's annotation was a type ALIAS of `str`. `field_layout_size`
  # reserved 8 bytes for the aliased field, so its sibling field landed exactly where the fat
  # field's own length half needed to go — a silent misread before crumb R3's two-half store
  # landed, a silent CORRUPTION of the sibling after it (R3 without R0).
  Scenario: alias_fat_field (own-native)
    Given source = "cases/alias_fat_field.tks"
    When built and run
    Then exit = 0

  Scenario: alias_fat_field (C route)
    Given source = "cases/alias_fat_field.tks"
    Given env = ["TEKO_BACKEND=c"]
    When built and run
    Then exit = 0

Feature: null-union member comparison — `a == null`/`a != null` (and a numeric-literal sibling) over an explicit `T | null` variant compile and give the right value on both routes (0.3.1.0, cargo/0.3.1.0-null-adopt)

  # `a != null` over an EXPLICIT `i32 | null` variant (owner's own literal example) compiled on
  # NEITHER route: the checker wrongly ACCEPTED it (`adopt_binop_operands` retyped the adopted bare
  # `null` to the WHOLE union instead of narrowing it to `Null`, `retype_literal`'s own correct arm),
  # so codegen honestly rejected the mistyped bare `null` downstream — "a bare `null` needs a known
  # optional type from context". A LANGUAGE gap (fails on both routes, no oracle to measure a fix
  # against), not a route-specific bug, so it belongs here rather than in a channel project.
  #
  # NOT placed in `examples/regressions/bulk` (the channel the R0 header above names for the F3
  # null-union matrix): that project is presently BROKEN on this branch for an unrelated reason —
  # its `main.tks`/`bulk.tkr` reference nine `q201`..`q209` defer modules (commit
  # `14fab92 fix(defer): panic/exit statements fire the scope's pending defer`) that do not exist
  # under its `src/`, so the whole project fails to build regardless of this change (reported, not
  # fixed here — a different vagão's concern). NOT placed in
  # `examples/regressions/own_native/src/corpus.tks` either: its `main.tks` exit-code budget (1-50,
  # reserving 42 for overall success) is already fully allocated across its existing `f_*` checks,
  # leaving no slot for a new top-level one.
  Scenario: variant_member_compare (own-native)
    Given source = "cases/variant_member_compare.tks"
    When built and run
    Then exit = 0

  Scenario: variant_member_compare (C route)
    Given source = "cases/variant_member_compare.tks"
    Given env = ["TEKO_BACKEND=c"]
    When built and run
    Then exit = 0
